haring (parallel for), reductions (reduction clause), scheduling (explicit schedule kind), thread granularity (collapse). The integral image approach naturally uses parallel for with collapse(2) on the search loops. For construction, we can use parallel for with static schedule and maybe collapse(1) or just simple.\n\nWe should also consider using tasks? But that might be overkill.\n\nGiven previous best score ~0.694, baseline 0.5, we want to improve further. Maybe by reducing overhead: combine row and column prefix sums into a single pass using parallel for with reduction on the integral image? Not straightforward.\n\nAnother idea: use OpenMP SIMD pragma in addition to work sharing? But role C is about OpenMP core constructs.\n\nLet's propose an experiment that uses collapse(2) on both construction (row-wise then column-wise) and search, but maybe adjust chunk size for dynamic schedule. Or try guided with small chunk.\n\nWe need to output JSON with hypothesis, transformations list, sources_kept empty array, files array containing the modified solution.cpp content, notes.\n\nHypothesis: \"Use integral image computed via two parallel prefix sum passes (row then column) each with static scheduling, and search all top-left corners using collapse(2) reduction(max) schedule(dynamic,16). This demonstrates OpenMP work sharing through parallel for directives on both construction and search phases, reductions via max reduction, explicit dynamic scheduling to balance irregular workload in the search phase, and thread granularity via collapsed loops.\"\n\nTransformations: [\"Parallelize row-wise prefix sum with static schedule\", \"Parallelize column-wise prefix sum with static schedule\", \"Use collapse(2) for nested loop over top-left corners\", \"Add reduction(max:mxSum)\", \"Set dynamic scheduling with chunk size 16\"]\n\nBut we need to ensure correctness. The integral image approach must handle large N up to 8000, S up to N-1.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <omp.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, s;\n    std::cin >> n >> s;\n    // Read matrix row by row using scanf for speed\n    std::vector<std::vector<int>> mat(n, std::vector<int>(n));\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &mat[i][j]);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Build integral image: prefix sums\n    std::vector<std::vector<long long>> pref(n+1, std::vector<long long>(n+1, 0));\n    \n    // Row-wise prefix sum (first pass)\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n        long long row_sum = 0;\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; ++j) {\n            row_sum += mat[i-1][j-1];\n            pref[i][j] = row_sum + pref[i-1][j]; // Actually need column prefix too, but we do two passes\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Column-wise prefix sum (second pass)\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) collapse(2)\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; ++j) {\n            pref[i][j] += pref[i-1][j]; // Wait, this would double count. Better approach: compute integral image in two steps.\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Actually standard integral image: first row prefix then column prefix.\n    // Let's do:\n    // Step 1: Row-wise cumulative sum (in-place on mat? but we need original for later? No, we can use pref)\n    // Better: compute pref[i][j] = mat[i-1][j-1] + pref[i-1][j] + pref[i][j-1] - pref[i-1][j-1]\n    // That's a single pass but with dependencies. We'll do two passes for parallelism.\n\n    // Let's redesign:\n```\n\nActually, the integral image can be computed in parallel by first doing row-wise prefix sums (each row independent) then column-wise prefix sums on that result (each column independent). This is correct and allows full parallelism.\n\nSo:\n\n```cpp\n// Step 1: Row-wise prefix sum into pref[i][j] = sum of mat[0..i-1][0..j-1]? No, we want cumulative per row.\nstd::vector<std::vector<long long>> row_pref(n+1, std::vector<long long>(n+1, 0));\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\nfor (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n    long long sum = 0;\n    for (int j = 1; j <= n; ++j) {\n        sum += mat[i-1][j-1];\n        row_pref[i][j] = sum;\n    }\n}\n\n// Step 2: Column-wise prefix sum on row_pref to get integral image\nstd::vector<std::vector<long long>> pref(n+1, std::vector<long long>(n+1, 0));\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) collapse(2)\nfor (int j = 1; j <= n; ++j) {\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n        pref[i][j] = row_pref[i][j] + pref[i-1][j];\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut note: collapse(2) on the column pass is fine because inner loop over i depends on previous i, but that's a reduction-like dependency. Actually it's a prefix sum along columns; each column can be done independently with serial prefix inside. So we should not use collapse(2) there because the inner loop has data dependency across iterations of i. We need to keep j as outer parallel and i sequential per thread. So:\n\n```cpp\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\nfor (int j = 1; j <= n; ++j) {\n    long long sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n        sum += row_pref[i][j];\n        pref[i][j] = sum;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut that's not exactly integral image. Actually the standard formula: integral(i,j) = mat[i-1][j-1] + integral(i-1,j) + integral(i,j-1) - integral(i-1,j-1). The two-pass method works if we first compute row prefix sums (cumulative per row), then column prefix sums on that result. Let's verify: After row-wise, row_pref[i][j] = sum_{k=0}^{j-1} mat[i-1][k]. Then after column-wise, pref[i][j] = sum_{r=0}^{i-1} row_pref[r+1][j] = sum_{r=0}^{i-1} sum_{k=0}^{j-1} mat[r][k] which is the integral image. Yes correct.\n\nSo we need to compute column prefix sums on row_pref, but each column j can be done independently with a serial loop over i (prefix). So no collapse needed there; just parallel for schedule(static) over columns.\n\nNow search phase: For all top-left corners (i,j) where 0 <= i <= n-s, 0 <= j <= n-s, compute sum = pref[i+s][j+s] - pref[i][j+s] - pref[i+s][j] + pref[i][j]. Then take max. This is a nested loop with independent iterations, perfect for collapse(2) reduction(max).\n\nWe'll use schedule(dynamic,16) to demonstrate dynamic scheduling.\n\nAlso we need to read input efficiently: using scanf in parallel might cause issues due to shared stdin? Actually reading from stdin in parallel can interleave lines incorrectly. Better to read sequentially first into a flat array or vector of vectors. We already have sequential reading but we used #pragma omp parallel for on the outer loop, which would cause multiple threads to call scanf concurrently - that's unsafe because scanf reads from shared stream and may produce garbled data. So we must keep input serial.\n\nThus: read matrix sequentially first (no OpenMP), then compute integral image in parallel, then search in parallel.\n\nLet's write final code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, s;\n    std::cin >> n >> s;\n    std::vector<std::vector<int>> mat(n, std::vector<int>(n));\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &mat[i][j]);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Build integral image using two-pass method\n    std::vector<std::vector<long long>> row_pref(n+1, std::vector<long long>(n+1, 0));\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n        long long sum = 0;\n        for (int j = 1; j <= n; ++j) {\n            sum += mat[i-1][j-1];\n            row_pref[i][j] = sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    std::vector<std::vector<long long>> pref(n+1, std::vector<long long>(n+1, 0));\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (int j = 1; j <= n; ++j) {\n        long long sum = 0;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n            sum += row_pref[i][j];\n            pref[i][j] = sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    int mxSum = -2147483648; // minimum possible\n    #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(dynamic,16)\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n-s; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 0; j <= n-s; ++j) {\n            long long sum = pref[i+s][j+s] - pref[i][j+s] - pref[i+s][j] + pref[i][j];\n            if (sum > mxSum) mxSum = sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", mxSum);\n}\n```\n\nBut we need to ensure that the reduction works with int and long long? The problem says result fits 32-bit signed integer, but intermediate sums may exceed. We used long long for pref, so sum is long long. But reduction variable mxSum is int; we should use long long for safety and cast at end. However OpenMP reduction requires same type. So change mxSum to long long.\n\nAlso note: The baseline uses sliding window which might be faster than integral image due to cache effects? Our previous experiments show integral image with collapse(2) gives ~0.06s vs baseline 0.13s, so it's good.\n\nWe need to include <omp.h> for OpenMP functions? Not necessary if we only use pragmas; but sometimes needed for omp_get_* etc. We don't call any functions, so no need.\n\nBut the compiler may require -fopenmp flag; that is handled by Makefile presumably.\n\nNow check correctness: The integral image formula uses pref[i][j] as sum of mat[0..i-1][0..j-1]. So submatrix from (i,j) to (i+s-1, j+s-1) has top-left corner (i,j). Sum = pref[i+s][j+s] - pref[i][j+s] - pref[i+s][j] + pref[i][j]. That's correct.\n\nEdge cases: s can be up to N-1. Loop bounds i <= n-s, j <= n-s inclusive? If i=n-s then submatrix ends at row n-1, so valid. So condition is i < n-s+1, i.e., i <= n-s. Same for j.\n\nWe used int mxSum = -2147483648; but better to initialize with first sum or use LLONG_MIN from climits. We'll include <climits> and set to LLONG_MIN.\n\nAlso we need to ensure that the reduction var